From: mh2lm clock work <clock@mh2lm>
Subject: [PATCH 0244] clock: place the NPU vote, then take it back

A voted branch is not enabled by its enable bit: the bit is a ballot, and the
branch runs only while at least one ballot is in the box. 0137 read the three
NPU branches the vendor declares BRANCH_VOTED and found all three holding
0x80000000 - vote clear, halt status set. Nothing else was voting, so this card
can produce one of the two things the aggregate half needs and not the other:

    our vote     -> the aggregate comes up          (this image can show it)
    our withdrawal -> the aggregate goes away        (only a second voter can
                                                      keep it up, and there is
                                                      none to observe)

The release row is therefore named detail=observed-not-proof, and the card claims
the vote and its own withdrawal. It does not claim CLK_OFF means the aggregate is
off: the NPU power collapse and RPMh are voters this driver cannot see. The
second-holder half stays open, and the probe is what will catch it if a later
boot ever reports bit 0 set at start.

Identity. The voted branch stays outside gccClocks[]: an eighth descriptor would
add a row to every image's decode listing, including the four images that never
map this page. The vote carries its own local ID and its own node name instead,
and the page is the fifth window 0137 already maps. localID 8 and vendor offset
0x008 are the whole identity.

Two stores, one guard. writeRegister() accepts the fifth window for one offset
only, and only when sweet,clock-vote armed it; writeVoteRegister() adds the
second check so the offset is named at the point of the store, not far from it.
The pass then follows the vendor tree's own budget: store, read back under the
configuration mask, poll CLK_OFF for 500 x 1 us, and on release wait the 10 us
the vendor's vote-disable delay asks for before reading the status again. A poll
that succeeds on its first read reports polls=0 - the field counts sleeps the
hardware needed, not reads the driver made.

Preconditions, because a vote is worth more than its own store. The register is
read before it is written, and two states are refused with zero stores:

    bit 0 already set          detail=vote-held-by-other
    halt status already clear  detail=halt-not-set

The first is somebody else's ballot: writing a 1 there erases nothing today but
the release would take back a bit this driver never placed. The second is an
aggregate that is up with no vote of ours in it - a state this card cannot
account for, and one a store would hide behind a successful-looking vote. Both
are refusals from the read, and both keep the value in the row as the evidence.

The release happens on every path after a successful store, including a poll that
ran out: a vote this pass no longer needs is a vote that keeps another master's
aggregate alive, and leaving it standing to make a timeout row look cleaner is
the one thing the card forbids. The verdict is kept as three answers instead of
one - vote-store= (the read-back showed only bit 0 moved), enable-done= (the
aggregate came up inside the budget) and settle= (the bit came back out and the
status read after it) - so a pass that voted and then saw the clock stall still
proves the store. A timeout returns kIOReturnTimeout, a failed read or a failed
release kIOReturnNotReady, and the start summary carries detail=vote-incomplete
with the result.

No ledger. The voted branch has no entry in gccClocks[], so it has no token to
acquire, and inventing one only to release it immediately would book the vote as
a demand this backend holds: ledgerRelease(token,false) on a last holder is the
"hardware-still-on" fault path, and a vote's normal outcome would read as that
fault. The vote keeps its own books - fVoteLeftSet and hold= on the done row -
and leaves the ledger describing the clocks this backend actually owns.

The generator puts the vote behind --clock-vote, which implies --clock-vote-probe
(a vote alone is a tree the backend refuses before the first map), and the ADT
test proves the tree is the probe tree plus exactly one property, removable back
to the probe bytes. Backward compatibility is the same rule as C2-03/04/05: an
image without sweet,clock-vote maps five windows, performs zero stores and
prints the 0137 start row unchanged - no vote row, no vote= field.

Host tests cover the happy path (two ordered stores, polls=0, one 10 us settle),
the two refusals, a vote whose aggregate never comes up (500 polls, the vote
still withdrawn, timings intact), the vote-without-probe and bad-value refusals,
and the probe-only ring staying at zero writes with no vote row at all.

